<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#TextualMaterial> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#prototypicalQuestions> "1. What written text appears in this meme? (Include all visible words, phrases, captions, and labels)\n\n2. How is the text positioned and formatted within the meme? (Consider placement, font size, color, style, and visual hierarchy)\n\n3. What is the tone and style of the written language used? (Formal/informal, serious/humorous, slang/standard, etc.)\n\n4. Are there any spelling, grammar, or punctuation choices that seem intentional? (Consider deliberate errors, unconventional capitalization, or punctuation for effect)\n\n5. What cultural references, phrases, or terminology are embedded in the text? (Identify idioms, pop culture references, internet slang, or specialized vocabulary)\n\n6. How does the text relate to or interact with the visual elements? (Does it complement, contradict, or add context to the images?)\n\n7. What assumptions does the text make about the audience's knowledge? (What background information or cultural literacy is assumed?)\n\n8. Are there multiple layers of meaning in the textual content? (Consider literal vs. implied meanings, wordplay, double entendres, or irony)\n\n9. What speech acts or communicative functions does the text perform? (Does it inform, persuade, mock, celebrate, complain, etc.?)\n\n10. How does the text contribute to the meme's overall message or humor? (What role does the written content play in achieving the meme's intended effect?)" .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#OverallIntent> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Overall Intent includes: communicative purposes, creator intentions, message goals, intended effects, purpose indicators, and intent signals."@en .
