<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01469#overallintent_instance> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#OverallIntent> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#MotionBlurIntegrated> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Text following movement paths and kinetic lines within dynamic visuals in memes"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#consilientWith> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#relationIndex> "7"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#TemporalRelation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Temporal Relation"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#verballyIronicalTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Indicates text saying the opposite of another dimension in memes, e.g. contradicting visual reality"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#entityOccluding> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#relationIndex> "15"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Scene> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Scene"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#speechThoughtIntegratedWith> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "speech thought integrated with"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#modallyContradicting> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "modally contradicting"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#selectivelyMasking> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#OverallIntent> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#TargetCommunity> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#promptExtractionText> "Extract Target Community elements from the image. Target Community includes: audience indicators, community markers, demographic cues, cultural references, community-specific elements, and target audience signals. For each community element found, provide: 1) A descriptive instance_name (e.g., 'internet_audience', 'meme_culture'), 2) A natural language label (e.g., 'the internet audience', 'the meme culture'), 3) A brief description of the intended audience or community. Focus on elements that indicate who the meme is designed for. Example output format: {\"instance_name\": \"internet_audience\", \"label\": \"the internet audience\", \"description\": \"The meme appears designed for internet-savvy audiences familiar with meme culture and dark humor\"}"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VerticalAdjacency> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Vertical Adjacency"@en .
