<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Scene> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#prototypicalQuestions> "1. How are the visual elements spatially distributed within the meme's frame? (Examine the placement, spacing, and positioning of all components across the visual space)\n\n2. What is the visual hierarchy and focal point of the composition? (Identify which elements draw attention first and how the eye moves through the scene)\n\n3. What action, situation, or scenario is taking place in this meme scene? (Identify and describe the events, interactions, or circumstances being depicted)\n\n4. How do foreground, middle ground, and background elements relate to each other? (Analyze depth layers and how different spatial planes interact or contrast)\n\n5. What geometric patterns or structural frameworks organize the scene? (Look for grids, symmetry, diagonal lines, triangular arrangements, or other compositional structures)\n\n6. How do size relationships between elements affect the scene's meaning? (Consider scale differences, proportional relationships, and how sizing choices impact interpretation)\n\n7. What directional flows or movement patterns are created within the composition? (Examine how visual elements guide attention or suggest motion through the scene)\n\n8. How is visual balance achieved or deliberately disrupted in the scene? (Analyze weight distribution, symmetry/asymmetry, and compositional stability or tension)\n\n9. How do overlapping or juxtaposed elements create meaning through their spatial relationships? (Examine what happens when elements intersect, contrast, or are placed in proximity)\n\n10. What role does negative space or empty areas play in the overall composition? (Analyze how unused space contributes to the scene's impact, readability, or aesthetic effect)" .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VerbalIrony> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Text saying the opposite of another dimension in memes, e.g. contradicting visual reality"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#DirectSuperimposition> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
