<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Semi-transparentOverlay> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#descriptivelyAlignedWith> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#relationIndex> "11"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#entityOccluding> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> _:n742c0716f0154719a847bf96a95b6370b25 .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01469#semiotic_projection_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The user is projected onto the overall violent metaphor scene, where the act of punching a Muslim child is equated with defusing a bomb. The user is implicitly positioned as the one performing the violent act ('you punch the muslim kid'), and the scene (the soldier, the text, the environment) serves as the context in which this act is framed as heroic or necessary. The user is not explicitly addressed with 'you' or 'me' in the text, but the first-person imperative and the violent, dehumanizing nature of the text create a direct projection of the user onto the aggressor role within the metaphor."@en .
